Othertext = """Currently, the question over who is considered an American, particularly within the context of immigration, has continued to be in the spotlight of politics today. The Trump administration finds the answer to this question is mass deportation of those who are dissimilar to them, while others hope to welcome them into the melting pot of this country. Both of these sides, whether unconsciously or consciously, assume that the foreigner is wrong and that Americans know better. We as Americans presume that we must give up our prior nationality in order to become American. These assumptions have severe impacts on immigrants because multiple cultures hold onto their birthplaces. Particularly to the Vietnamese, home is culturally important (Shrivasta et al 3). After the Vietnam war, refugees felt disheartened that they had to flee to the country, America, that had failed to win them the war. 
Written decades later, Viet Thanh Nguyen’s The Sympathizer investigates the omnipresent effects of the Vietnam War on society today. Nguyen, after writing as a critic for years, published The Sympathizer in 2015 in order to “hold everyone accountable” for the Vietnam War (Nguyen 395). The story surrounds an unnamed main character who, as a North Vietnamese spy undercover as the captain to the General, writes a confession about everything that occurred after the fall of Saigon. The confession starts with the narrator escaping Vietnam and arriving in America. Then the narrator recalls past assignments in Vietnam and America that he executed for the North or South Vietnamese. After describing the narrator’s return to Vietnam and capture, the novel then shifts to the present narrator having to argue with a commandant over his writing. The narrator then receives torture until he gains an awakening into his life and the war itself and flees Vietnam once more with a new found hope. Given the plot, Nyugen uses The Sympathizer to expose readers to problems associated with identity for the victim and aggressor of colonial practices by mirroring modern day remnants of this post-colonial era with views of immigrants and the inaccurate depiction of the Vietnam War, alterations between sentence structure, unique novel structure, and versatile use of figurative language, which assists readers in understanding a contrary perspective to the Vietnam War. 
Structure wise, Viet Thanh Nguyen adopts the style of a confession to hook American readers into the novel. Americans enjoy confessions, which is why they are popular in the United States (Panigrahi 43-44). This is because Americans are somewhat interested in criminal perspectives. Additionally, Confessions make the meaning of words important (Panigrahi). Therefore, readers analyze why the confessor used specific literary choices when writing their confession. By making The Sympathizer resemble a confession, Nguyenn attracts an American audience and pulls them into the story. Furthermore, the role of the narrator as a spy also causes readers to examine the text. In the first lines of the book the speaker states, “I am a spy, a sleeper, a spook, a man of two faces”(Nguyen). Two faced is a common expression in America and stands as a metaphor for an untruthful person. The narrator’s occupation as a spy means he likely does not tell the truth too. Especially in America, the majority of American society views spies as back stabbing and immoral figures due to the history of the CIA and overall distrust of the government’s morality. This makes readers investigate the content of the text to determine its validity, which draws readers into looking for the deeper meaning of the text.
In order to understand the meaning of The Sympathizer, readers must first understand the postcolonial lens. Postcolonization is a literary lens that investigates how colonialism affects past colonies. Typically, post colonialists investigate how a colonized group is caught in between two different groups, which leads them to be unaccepted in both groups and lack a self image (Schilb et Clifford 276). In other words, postcolonization tries to view the effects of past colonization on a person’s identity. Another lens that readers must know is deconstructionism. The definition of deconstructionism is that words do not inherently have positive or negative connotations to them, but due to people’s biases and Western thought, words are separated into opposing categories (Schilb et Clifford 275). In other words, Western society has given words set meanings and removed nuanced definitions. Both definitions must be understood because a combination of the two lenses should be used to interpret The Sympathizer due to their reliance on one another. Deconstructionism relies on Westerners' influence on the meaning of words, which derives from colonial practices. Post colonialism also plays off of the deconstructionist ideas about bias and different meanings. Therefore, combining lenses help readers best analyze the meaning of The Sympathizer. 
 Although America did not technically colonize Vietnam, the act should still be considered colonization because America imposed multiple of their influences on Vietnam. One of these influences was making South Vietnam reliant on American weapons. This is clearly depicted when the narrator states, “what would we have done with that money? Buy the ammunition, gas, and spare parts for the weapons planes, and tanks the same Americans had bestowed on us… Having given us the needles, they now perversely no longer supplied the dope” (Nguyen 4). The comparison between needles and tanks is not only a reference to the Opium Wars, Britain trying to make the Chinese reliant on Opium in order to trade, but also a depiction of how reliant America had made Vietnam. By comparing the Vietnam War to the Opium Wars, Nguyen directly connects America with colonization. 
Additionally, readers see power structures constructed around Americans being considered more important than Vietnamese. As described during the narrator’s initial escape from Vietnam, the Vietnamese were packed into tight buses while Americans were given better treatment (Nguyen 41). Treating Americans better than Vietnamese hints at a deep prejudice that exists between the two, which suggests Americans consider themselves superior to Vietnamese. This mirrors Social Darwinism, a philosophy that Europe adopted to excuse colonization. Social Darwinism took Darwin’s ideas about evolution and applied them to humans. Europe used this perspective to excuse their colonization because they viewed the colonized as inferior and they, the Europeans, as superior. Using this flawed logic, Europe asserted that it was natural to colonize and force the colonized subjects to resemble their European society. In a similar manner, America mirrors Europe’s approach by valuing Americans as superior to Vietnamese in order to excuse their involvement in the Vietnam War, and violations against the Vietnamese people. 
Similarly, Viet Thanh Nguyen’s confessional structure shows the reader the effects of colonial practices. The novel being a confession means the narrator does not truly have the power of representation. Throughout the novel, the commandant and his edits are referenced. For example, at the beginning of the fifth chapter, the narrator says “bearing in mind your notes on what I have confessed thus far, I suspect, my dear Commandant, that this confession is not what you are most likely used to reading” (Nguyen 72). The commandant is making changes and influencing the narrator to alter his confession to support his own beliefs. These edits mirror how imperials take away representation from the colonized because the commandant has final say over the narrator's confession and uses his power to adjust the confession to support his own view.
Moreover, the style itself symbolizes the importance of owning your own depiction. Because the commandant could have edited certain parts of the novel, the confessional style of the novel directly portrays the authority of colonies over representation(Panigrahi 76). Therefore, Nguyen’s use of a confessional style itself argues against removing one’s ability to accurately portray themselves. By controlling the confession, the commandant and Communist regime hold power over the narrator and alter his confession for their benefit. The saying “History is written by the victors” mirrors this idea. In this case, the conquerors (colonizers) are the ones who control the power of representation while the conquered (colonized) are unable to. Historically, Europeans, like the Spanish, prevented Native Americans from practicing their indigenous religions and silenced their cultural practices. Similar to the colonizers before them, the Communist regime prevents the narrator from writing what he wants to, and alters specific words and language to favor their view of the world. Altering recounts of events has been used throughout American history. In some American states, textbooks have been altered to tone down the violence and moral evil of slavery and the slave trade. This caused those taught at these schools to have an inaccurate understanding of these events and, in a sense, alter history. 
Furthermore, the literary language and symbolism of the female agent’s fate throughout the novel explains why people change the truth. One very important mystery in the novel, whose truth does not get revealed until near the end, is of what occurred to the female agent with the list of names. Readers are first introduced to this incident near the beginning of the book, where the narrator cuts away before exploring what happened. He describes how he “saw the truth in her eyes, and the truth was that she hated me for what she thought I was… Three years later… I kept her folder on my desk, a reminder of my failure to save her” (Nguyen 9-10). The narrator has guilt for his inaction, but, in order to move on, he hides the true cause of what happened. He ignores the true event in order to escape blame. 
Near the end of the book, Man reveals that the narrator has been hiding the truth behind what happens to the Northern Vietnamese agent. Man mentions this to the narrator during the torture: “You mention her four more times in your confession… but we don’t learn her fate… how is her tragedy possible to forget?” (Nguyen 336). The narrator’s purposeful misremembrance shows that the narrator is hiding something from the reader, his torturer, and, most importantly, himself. This suggests that people repress memories in order to escape the responsibility of their actions and remove the guilt they have. Instead of accepting his inaction, the narrator lies to himself in order to escape his role in the atrocity. These lies continue until the narrator reveals that the women was raped into confession as he grimly watched (Nguyen 349-350). The reveal comes as a shock to readers, and helps explain why the narrator did not want to reveal what happened. In fact, the narrator writes about this event in the third person stating, “the commissar would play the patient a tape recording of his answer… He heard this stranger’s voice say, I saw everything” (Nguyen 349). Nguyen’s switch from a first to third person perspective symbolizes the narrator separating themselves from their responsibility in the situation. The repression of this memory occurred because the narrator wanted to remove the guilt he had (Shrivasta et al 13). Therefore, Shrivasta’s explanation creates a better understanding of why the narrator hid the truth from himself. 
Supporting Shrivasta’s analysis is the presence of the narrator’s guilt in the structure of the narrator's description. By containing powerful imagery and euphemisms, Nguyen makes the reader decide whether or not to look deeper into the meaning of the euphemisms or blissfully ignore it (Panigrahi 91). These literary devices cause the reader to mirror the narrator’s purposeful ignorance. Does the reader ignore the details of the event to protect themselves from discomfort, like the narrator in his confession, or does the reader accept the events, like the narrator in the recording? The self reflection that readers experience makes them understand why nations, organizations, and people alter and change what happened. Humans have a desire to hide their guilt. 
Equally important is the novel’s exploration of the problems associated with the identity of refugees and post-colonial subjects. Although the novel deals with mostly Vietnamese refugees in America, one of the characters, Ms Mori, is a Japanese American born citizen. In the novel, she laments how the Department Chair expected her to hold onto her Asian culture and, because she never felt interested in it, she felt guilty (Nguyen 75). This shows that society expects Ms Mori and others to hold onto their roots. She must preserve her history or risk judgement. At a Vietnamese wedding, Ms Mori challenges, “Did anyone ask John F. Kennedy if he spoke Gaelic and visited Dublin… isn’t my culture right here since I was born here?”(Nguyen 75). Comparing Ms Mori and JFK shows the double standard Americans have in place for good or bad intentions. Both are American born citizens, but Americans view Ms Mori as a foreigner who is not sticking to her role. Americans identify second year refugees as foreign even though the second year refugee thinks they are American (Shrivasta et al 8-9). Shrivasta’s analysis is supported by America’s clear cut definitions on identity. People are either American or foreign and there is no in between. Although this does contain deconstructionist thought, it also offers an explanation for postcolonial discussion on the struggle with identity. Refugees, like Ms Mori, do not have an identity because they feel like they do not belong to either group: Americans do not accept them, and they do not resemble or understand their culture enough to be accepted there either. 
In a similar manner, the novel portrays refugees’ struggle to cling onto their identity and their inability to. One of the struggles Vietnamese refugees face in the novel is the deterioration of their national pride and culture. Depicting the lack of fish sauce in the U.S is portrayed in The Sympathizer as a struggle for the refugees to maintain their culture (Shrivasta et al 8). The replacement of Vietnamese food with Chinese food supports Shrivasta’s analysis. In fact, the narrator states how such a decision was “another blow in the gauntlet of our humiliation that left us with the sweet-and-sour taste of unreliable memories… correct enough to invoke the past, just wrong enough to remind us that the past was forever gone” (Nguyen 70). By referencing the flavor of Chinese cuisine, Nguyen makes a comparison between the sweet-and-sour taste of the food and the effect the food had on refugees’ memory of their culture. Nguyen’s use of such a comparison acts as a way for readers to understand the feelings of the refugees. The hint of their culture makes them miss their nation, but the differences in the flavor remind them they no longer can visit it. This shows that refugees want to identify as Vietnamese, but are unable to in America. 
Moreover, the refugees cannot look toward the future and become American. The refugees in the novel are unable to move on because they are trapped in the past without an identity (Stanley 284). Stanley’s analysis is supported by the text itself. For instance, the narrator notices that the clock at Madame’s restaurant is set to the wrong time and is actually synced with Saigon time (Nguyen 199). The clock is still set to the time of their city, which shows they have not moved on from their past lives. Furthermore, the narrator adds that “if one judged time by this clock, it was we who were fourteen hours off… we did not simply live in two cultures… people also lived in two time zones, the here and there, … we were only going in circles” (Nguyen 199). These comparisons between time zones, past and present, represent the separation the Vietnamese refugees have made. They refuse to assimilate with Americans in order to hold onto their culture, but they also lose their culture because they cannot live in Vietnam. The narrator points out that he and refugees go through a cycle, as referenced by going in circles, of despair and identitylessness. The narrator also suggests that the simile "there's something fishy around here” derives from American’s judgemental view on Vietnamese culture (Nguyen 70). This meaning reveals that the simile’s purpose is to identify and separate Vietnamese from themselves by associating the fish sauce smell with suspicion. Americans prevent these refugees from assimilating because they group the Vietnamese and their culture as a negative. Therefore, these refugees are left without an identity. 
An additional idea explored in the novel is the barriers that society places on the narrator, and how these boundaries influence his self identity. The narrator is not trusted or seen as Communist, a group he thinks he belongs to. One example of this is when he tries to reveal the truth about himself to Sonny, who he thinks is a Communist. When the narrator is sent to assassinate Sonny, he tells him that he is a Communist, but Sonny does not believe him, due to the narrator talking with the South Vietnamese General, and determines the narrator is trying to trick him (Nguyen 275). This is an unusual behaviour by the narrator because he is supposed to be undercover. The narrator explains that he told Sonny his true identity because “I cannot be the only one who believes that if others just saw who I really was, then I would be understood… But what would happen if one took off the mask and the other saw one not with love but with horror” (Nguyen 275).  His desire to be understood by someone he deems an ally led to why he revealed his identity.  Yet, Sonny does not welcome the narrator. Instead he looks upon the narrator with terror. This destroys the narrator’s identity as a Communist because those around him do not believe him nor accept him. 
Furthermore, these ideas are supported by the reeducation guard’s refusal to refer to him as a comrade. After being captured as part of a South Vietnamese rebel mission and reeducated, the narrator faces another attack on his identity. The narrator, while being tortured, begs a guard to let him sleep: “Please, Comrade… just let me sleep. The baby-faced guard snorted, … and said, I’m not your comrade” (Nguyen 338). The clear decline by the guard to refer to him as comrade supports the idea that the narrator is not identified by his compatriots as Communist, despite his personal identification as such. Both of the narrator's rebukes show a pattern. Sonny does not believe the narrator is Communist because the narrator interacts with the South Vietnamese General, so Sonny assumes he must be lying. As for the guard and the camp itself, because they caught the narrator in a South Vietnamese rebel group, they assume that the narrator must not be Communist. This shows that society assumes people can only be a part of one group, which hints at deconstructionist thought. Group conflicts are also represented in the novel by his two friends. Toward the beginning of the novel, the narrator states how his two blood brothers Bon, a Southern soldier, and Man, a Northern soldier, are on polar opposite sides of the war (Nguyen 3 10-11). These polar opposites trap the narrator in a moral struggle between helping only one side because he cannot assist one without hurting the other. Therefore, the narrator is made to be identityless because he is unable to identify as a Communist or Southern Vietnamese due to the society around him. 
Building off of this idea, the treatment of the narrator by society causes him to become a spy. One of the reasons behind the narrator’s acceptance as a mole (spy) derives from the narrator’s mixed race causing him to be ostracized by society (Shrivasta et al 10). This analysis is supported by the novel’s text. The novel relates the narrator and mole by comparing the mole on his face to the animal who “was surely ugly to all except its own mother” (Nguyen 174). Ngyuens comparison of the narrator's imperfection, his mole on his face, to the animal being seen as ugly mirrors the narrator’s mixed race. Colonial and Vietnamese society view the narrator’s mixed race as an imperfection, like a facial mole. Readers can also see how the narrator would be subjected to roles that are hated. In India, the Chandalas live at the bottom of the social hierarchy and are forced to work jobs considered impure. Chandala occupations involve handling corpses or other immoral Hindu acts. Somewhat similar to the Chandalas, society’s prejudice toward the narrator forced him to become a spy. 
Another reason the narrator’s mixed race causes him to be a spy is because it helps him fulfill his role. As Man explains to the narrator, “A spy’s task is to hide where everyone can see him and where he can see everything” (Nguyen 174). By belonging to no defined group, the narrator is not seen as part of the in or out group. Instead, he is overlooked by the world. Specifically, a white man does not pay attention to the narrator because the narrator is not white (Nguyen 278). His disregard is because the narrator is seen as Asian by the American. This supports Kerishma Panigrahi’s analysis that the narrator’s mixed race allows him to be able to disappear into the crowd (68). Therefore, the mixed race of the narrator allows him to belong to no group and fulfill the role of a spy. The narrator’s split sympathies to both causes allows him to appear as one or the other too. The narrator introduces himself as a man who can view either side (Nguyen 1). These split sympathies allowed him to play the role as a captain for the South Vietnamese General, and an insider for the North Vietnamese. 
Overall, the narrator is isolated by the world’s perception of him. Throughout the book, despite being educated in American ways, and becoming a refugee to America, the narrator is never fully accepted as an American. As Ms Mori points out in the confession, “It doesn’t matter how long you’ve been here… White people will always think we’re foreigners” (Nguyen 120). Mori’s blatant statement of Americans regarding immigrants and refugees as foreigners shows that the narrator is not welcomed into the American identity. As stated before by the narrator himself,  “the majority of Americans regarded us with ambivalence… we being living reminders of their defeat” (Nguyen 117). Therefore, the narrator, despite being interested in going to America, is not accepted as an American due to his Vietnameseness. 
Specifically, the narrator’s mixed race causes him to be isolated by the Vietnamese. Because of his French and Vietnamese blood, the narrator is not seen as Vietnamese (Stefan 219). This analysis is supported by multiple examples in the novel. One of these examples is when the General tells the narrator he would never let him, a bastard, date his daughter (Nguyen 291). The general, due to the narrator’s mixed race, thinks the narrator is considered to not be Vietnamese enough, and prevents him from dating his daughter, which illustrates society’s ostracizing view on the narrator. Another example is the narrator’s perception of his birthplace. During an argument with Man over going to America, the narrator says how he does not belong in Vietnam (Nguyen 29-30). This depicts the narrator’s struggle with identifying as Vietnamese. He does not feel welcome in his birthplace yet wants to be, but society prevents him from doing so. 
Correspondingly, the narrator's indecision to be either Vietnamese or American causes him to not have an identity. As the Department Chair points out to the narrator, “the Amerasian, forever caught between worlds and never knowing where he belongs… feeling the constant tug-of-war inside you”( Nguyen 63). Nguyen argues that within the societal structures imperialistic nations have created, those who identify as more than one group are unaccepted in both, which is why post-colonial subjects suffer from identity crisis. They are unable to return to their old culture and assimilate into the new one. If they do, they will not be perceived as either because society assumes that identities are boolean (in the group or not in the group). The narrator is either Vietnamese or American. This means subjects are stuck between the two without the opportunity to be both. 
Furthermore, the novel portrays the challenges with trying to abandon the colonial system’s effects. The narrator has difficulty identifying himself without the structure colonialism established (Panigrahi 47). Panigrahi’s analysis is upheld by the novel. When the Auteur shows up to set, he wants to be with the Vietnamese extras, but he thinks doing so separates him from the white movie makers (Nguyen 156). The narrator, due to the influences of his society, feels he can only be a part of one of these groups. Eventually, the narrator decides to abandon the Vietnamese extras in order to have advantages not offered to them. The narrator explains, “the sight of an authentic outhouse stirred neither any sentimental feelings in me nor any admiration any admiration… I preferred a flush toilet… If longing for these riches made me an Occidentalist, I confess to it” (Nguyen 150). The narrator’s choice to abandon his stand with the extras, who would have to use the outhouse, leads readers to believe he does not identify as Vietnamese else he would stand up for his people. His disidentification is based on Western thought where humans are either in or out of a group. 
Throughout the novel, the narrator is aware of the double meanings of words. One of these examples is Ms Mori’s slogan analogy. In the analogy, Ms Mori states how “A slogan is just an empty suit… Anyone can wear it” (Nguyen 120). With a deconstructionist lens, readers find that Ms Mori is arguing that words can take on different meanings depending on the person who uses them. Furthermore, a few paragraphs later, the narrator states that “the best kind of truth [is] the one that meant at least two things” (Nguyen 121). This shows the narrator himself believes words differ based on the user. Likewise, his role as a spy makes his words adopt two meanings: one of irony and the other of honesty. From the perspective of the narrator as a Northern Vietnamese agent, his lies appear ironic, but when seen from the perspective of a South Vietnamese sympathizer, his phrases seem genuine. The Department Chair brings up a similar idea with his conversation with the narrator. In the conversation, the narrator asks the Department Chair if he must achieve balance between his two sides like the yin and yang (Nguyen 65).  Although the question itself is not significant, Nguyen’s symbol of the yin and yang is. Yin and yang is used to contrast the ideas of Western philosophers who dealt in absolutes. In this case, Nguyen is presenting the idea that Asian society and culture held more deconstructionist beliefs until Europeans colonized them. All of this helps prove how the narrator held onto this view in the beginning, but due to colonialism lost it. 
This altered perception is shown in the confession’s word choice too. The narrator during his conversation with the commissar, is criticized for the lack of Vietnam allusions and his preference to use American allusions (Nguyen 318-319). Nguyen’s mass use of American allusions over others hint that the narrator has forgotten his Vietnamese culture and roots. Sandra Stanley supports this with her analysis that the narrator’s use of American allusions over Vietnamese ones shows that the narrator has been reeducated by the United States (286). In other words, Nguyen’s use of allusions and his structure as a confession allude to the narrator’s bias and helps readers understand the effects that society can have on influencing who people become. By having the narrator differ from his cultural roots, it shows how postcolonialism and society has changed who he is.
Beyond the colonial themes depicted, the novel expands these points to reality. Nguyen uses allusions to refer to how America, particularly Hollywood, misrepresents the history of American conflicts. In one part of the book the narrator decides to help oversee a movie about the Vietnam War that portrays the Vietnamese as savages or hostages (Nguyen 125-126). This inaccurate representation is more than just a broad criticism of Hollywood. It serves as a reference to a specific example. According to an analysis conducted by Sandra Stanley, the film in the book the narrator works on, The Hamlet, is a euphemism for the film Apocalypse Now, which portrays white Americans as human and Vietnamese as inhuman (291). Nguyen uses this euphemism to help convey to Americans their colonial past by referencing a film. Americans have rewritten the history of the ones they colonized and portrayed them in a negative light in order to support their preexisting beliefs. As Kerishma Panigrahi asserts, “the Vietnam War becomes a site upon which settler colonial logics are not merely exported but actively recycled” (65). This perspective about the novel describing the United States’ white washing of history is further supported by another example in the novel. After talking to the Auteur, the narrator realizes that Hollywood was only an efficient way to exploit Vietnam, mirroring French colonists (Nguyen 134). Even after escaping colonization, Vietnam is still abused and misrepresented. The difference now, as Sandra Stanley asserts, is that “the soft power of the culture industry can trump the hard power of the military complex” (291). America can still have control over Vietnam by misrepresenting them and controlling the public’s view. These movies and inaccuracies are observed by others and continue the same dehumanizing views of the colonial past. 
Similarly, Nguyen throughout the novel depicts reeducation as a key part in the book. For example, Americans expected the narrator to speak broken English due to his race (Nguyen 7). Americans in the novel are surprised to see the narrator speak English correctly because of the stereotypes that Vietnamese are inferior and ignorant. These ideas are based on the colonial view of the uneducated Vietnamese refugees being saved by Western Education, which the story investigates (Ma 12-13). In other words, America views itself as a savior for the Vietnamese immigrants, which mirrors Europe’s excuse for colonization. By making this point in the novel, Nguyen expands this argument to the English literary canon, which forces Vietnamese writers to reeducate themselves and follow English norms to be successful (Ma 18-19). Typical depictions of the Vietnamese War in America have not been critical of America. Thus Vietnamese writers likely follow the norm America has put in place. English writers and literary institutions have also expected authors to follow patterns and rules that have been set by themselves, and to be successful immigrant authors must follow the precedent set before them. 
Adding onto these references, Nguyen relates the novel to torture practices the Americans used that mirror colonial practices. Whether it is the narrator following the CIA’s protocols, the torture the narrator witnessed, or the narrator himself being tortured, the novel argues that this torture derives from colonial influences. Nguyen then ties these moments and relates them back to America and their influences. One example of torture in the novel is the narrator’s interrogation of the Watchman. The narrator, as a CIA interrogator, tells the Watchman his rights, which the Watchman quickly critiques, “to them [Americans] all yellow people are guilty until proven innocent… they believe in a universe of divine justice… but they also believe in a secular justice where human beings are presumed innocent” (Nguyen 187-190). The Watchman’s critique serves two main points. The first is that Americans, despite presuming innocence for their own kind, do not offer the same human rights to the foreigner. This is because Americans see the foreigner as lesser than them, which mirrors colonial ideologies. In other words, the Watchman’s critique on the U.S symbolizes how the U.S considers people who are not American as lesser than (Stefan 227). 
The second point the Watchman makes is the point that they use this excuse to torture Vietnamese like him. The U.S uses the racial disparity of the accused as reasoning to presume their guilt and torture them (Stefan 223, 227). This follows similar patterns of behaviour in America today, particularly the deportation of immigrants without due process. The Trump administration assumes that these people are not worthy enough to have due process because they consider them as foreign. Additionally, the Watchman’s torture mirrors one more practice of the U.S. After having trouble getting a confession from him, the narrator blackmails the Watchman with a fake confession that reasons the Watchman joined the revolution because he is gay (Nguyen 191). Although such blackmail might not be as impactful as in America, in Vietnam (at the time of the book) it was seen as disgraceful. The narrator's abuse of a cultural taboo in order to get the Watchman to confess shows how colonies abuse the culture of the nation they are conquering to gain power. In this case, the narrator gains power by exploiting Vietnam’s and the Watchman’s view on homosexuality. These practices mirror consistent tactics the U.S used against Arabs during the War on Terror and were written in torture manuals used in the Vietnam War (Stefan 221-222). This means the novel’s descriptions of torture mechanisms relates readers to the reality. America takes advantage of the culture of other nations in order to control them. Hayley Stefan also points out that these practices create the “assumptions of shame is predicated upon race-based rhetoric that deems… all racially Brown victims, homophobic and transphobic…  this move to torture based on supposedly deviant sexual behaviour reaffirms the moral superiority of American torturers” (Stefan 221-222). These practices therefore create a cycle of Americans identifying themselves as morally superior and strengthen the assumption that foreigners oppose sexualities that differ from hetero. 
Expanding on the effects of colonization, the novel explores the theme that once a country is colonized it creates a cycle of suffering. One key aspect of the novel examines the continuing oppression of people in Vietnam after the American occupation. This idea is hinted out throughout, but is more prominent at the end of the novel. Toward the end of the narrator’s interrogation, Man explains that “now that we [the North Vietnamese] are the powerful, we don’t need the French or the Americans to fuck us over. We can fuck ourselves just fine” (Nguyen 364). The expletives from Man, who as a Northern Vietnamese intellectual and mentor to the narrator does not typically curse, shows how he is upset with how the revolutionaries repeated history.  Even by removing the colonizer from the colony, colonial power structures persist and push the natives of the colony to continue the cycle.
Man’s statement is further explained by the narrator after having reached a realization. The narrator states, “our revolution had gone from being the vanguard of political change to the rear guard hoarding power… When it came to learning the worst habits of our French masters and their American replacements, we quickly proved ourselves the best”(Nguyen 376). Despite its revolutionary beginnings, the Vietnamese became a colonial-like nation by adopting the practices of the French and Americans. This reflects Man’s statement about how the Vietnamese can oppress themselves without a need for a foreign power. The narrator offers an explanation for why this occurred by asking, “Hadn’t the French and Americans done exactly the same? Once revolutionaries themselves, they had become imperialists… taking away our freedoms in the name of saving us” (Nguyen 376). North Vietnam was no different from the colonizers before them. All three nations built themselves off of revolutionary ideals about human rights and freedom from the oppressed, but all three became oppressors. The transformation of these nations shows how colonialism is a process that feeds itself. 
Finally, the novel explores the double meaning of nothing and how it solves effects of colonialism. Towards the end of the novel, after being kept awake for a long time, the narrator makes a realization. As to his realization, the narrator explains that “while nothing is more precious than independence and freedom, nothing is also more precious than independence and freedom!” (Nguyen 375). Nguyen’s use of italics in this sentence is to make the reader interpret the sentence differently in order to understand the second meaning of nothing. His paradoxical idea explores the fact that the absence of something is often more important than the presence of something.  Additionally, the quote directly correlates with deconstructionism, which believes that these words have no meaning, so nothing can mean something. The narrator applies this idea by stating, “a revolution fought for independence and freedom could make those things worth less than nothing” (Nguyen 376). In this example, the narrator implies that the North Vietnamese, after fighting for freedom, made the values they fought for worthless. 
Structure wise, the novel explores these dual identities and how they can be a solution to post-colonial impacts by altering the point of view grammatically. Normally, the book adopts a purely first person perspective, due to its structure as a confession, but once the narrator is tortured extensively, the novel switches to a third person perspective. The narrator begins referring to himself as “the patient” and the use of “he” replaces the typical “I” subject (Nguyen 339-349). These changes in the novel’s normal structure and diction bring emphasis to the narrator seeing himself as the world sees him. Before this point in the novel, the reader only saw the narrator’s own personal view of himself. Therefore, this change in perspective causes readers to contrast the narrator depicted in the confession to the one depicted previously. By utilizing this literary technique, Nguyen makes readers recognize the differences between the morality of the narrator, and Nguyen helps build the narrator's dual identity. 
Continuing this pattern, the narrator’s perspective also takes on a plural first person sentence structure. The sentence structure changes after the narrator states, “I was that man of two minds, me and myself” (Nguyen 376). This is an important transition because it shows the narrator sees himself in two different ways. One example of this structure is the narrator describing his final interaction with Man: “We tried to refuse it, but he slipped the book in the rucksack and pressed it on us” (Nguyen 376). The novel’s final perspective returns to the initial sentence structure but alters the pronouns referring to himself to reflect the singular plural form. The narrator uses “we” to combine self perception and public perception because the narrator identifies himself differently (Panigrahi 106). This makes the narrator aware of the definitions that are placed onto him, but also aware of his own portrayal of himself, which leads to the narrator recognizing his own biases and those around him. By adopting this dual identity, the narrator escapes the imperialistic idea that individuals belong to only one group (Stanley 296-298). The narrator, after being trapped by society, finally able to have an identity. 
Given all of its expansive content, it is clear to see why The Sympathizer won a Pulitzer prize and positively contributed to the literary canon. With unique novel structure and style, Nguyen significantly contributes to the literary canon through his unique and critical perspective of the Vietnam War. By using a confessional novel structure, Nguyen hooks American readers into the novel. In addition, with the help of a post-colonialist and deconstructionist lens, readers get an understanding about how and why those from past colonies suffer from identitylessness and are given a solution to the loss of identity for immigrants and refugees. In all, Nguyen’s The Sympathizer acts as an overseer over past and current colonial practices not only in America but the world itself. Utilizing unique literary elements (structure, figurative language, and diction), Nguyen assists the reader in understanding the struggles of immigrants and the different perceptions of oneself.  """
